Sval: a toplevel window on Wayland asks the compositor to blur what is behind it

A see-through window shows what is behind it through a backdrop the system
draws: the HUD material on macOS, Acrylic on Windows, asked for by the
root's windowsmica attribute through nsIWidget::SetMicaBackdrop. On Linux
the compositor offers blur through ext-background-effect-v1, which KDE
Plasma 6.7 and GNOME 51 carry. The GTK widget binds it, a toplevel window
asks for blur on its surface while windowsmica is set, and
-moz-windows-mica reports whether the compositor blurs, so the chrome
offers see-through only where it shows blurred.

